# Workspace access and MCP authorization

Status: proposed implementation contract. This document makes the remaining design calls so implementation can proceed while the owner is away. [brief.md](brief.md) remains the source of truth for owner decisions; the assumptions below are proposals, not additional owner decisions. The repository currently contains no MCP handlers or library implementation to validate against.

## Product decisions

- Connect an agent once per Alderdocs account and OAuth client. The grant covers the person's accessible documents across workspaces; it is not tied to an active workspace or one provider organization.
- Open the library on **All documents**, including documents shared directly with the person. Workspace selection filters this collection; it never changes account authority.
- Every create operation names its destination workspace explicitly. Neither browser session state nor the agent's last-used workspace supplies a destination.
- Authorize every operation against current access at the shared product boundary. OAuth consent limits delegated powers; it never creates membership or document access.

These choices address the brief's two reported problems: reconnecting an agent for each client and losing track of documents or creating them in the wrong workspace. Workspace boundaries still own documents, memberships, storage accounting, and administration.

## Identity, ownership, and access records

The managed auth provider owns stable user identities and organization memberships and roles. Each workspace maps to exactly one provider organization, including the workspace created at sign-up. That first workspace has no privileged fallback behavior. The application owns document-to-workspace relationships, direct document shares, OAuth consent records, and the capability policy.

Use stable opaque application IDs for users, workspaces, and documents. Names and email addresses are labels or invitation inputs, never mutation identities. Each document has exactly one immutable `workspace_id` under these tools. Updating content cannot move a document, change its owner, or share it.

An application membership mirror is a cache of provider truth, not an independent membership system. Process verified webhooks idempotently and reconcile missed events. Permission-sensitive requests must use a current authoritative membership check or a provider-supported freshness/revocation mechanism with an explicitly tested bound. Do not ship an indefinite webhook-only cache as immediate revocation. If current authority cannot be established, fail closed for the affected operation rather than treating an old token role as current membership.

Direct shares bind one document to one stable user after the provider verifies the invited email. A pending email invitation grants no access. Resolve the invitation to the verified subject; do not match arbitrary request email strings on each read or transfer access when an account changes its email. A direct share grants no workspace membership, workspace browsing, member list, or sibling document access.

### Capability matrix

| Access path | Discover/read document | Update content | Create in workspace | Manage workspace/members/shares |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Workspace Owner | Yes, all documents in workspace | Yes | Yes | Owner administration, outside these MCP tools |
| Workspace Editor | Yes, all documents in workspace | Yes | Yes | No |
| Workspace Viewer | Yes, all documents in workspace | No | No | No |
| Accepted direct share, without membership | Yes, that document only | No | No | No |
| No access | No | No | No | No |

Direct shares are read-only in this proposal because the brief does not specify an editing role for them. If direct editing is later approved, add an explicit share capability; never infer it from possession of a link. Workspace members retain the powers of their role even if they also have a read-only direct share. Losing membership leaves access only when an independent accepted share remains valid.

Use named capabilities `documents.read`, `documents.update`, `documents.create`, and `workspaces.discover`. The last permits discovering workspaces of which the person is a member, not every workspace containing a directly shared document. Document listing uses `documents.read` for each returned document.

## One account-wide OAuth grant

Use one canonical MCP resource and endpoint for the product, with standard managed-provider OAuth. Bind tokens to the authenticated subject, client, resource, approved scopes, and a revocable grant. Authenticate the subject independently of a selected organization. An organization claim may help resolve membership, but it is neither an account-wide access proof nor a constraint that silently reduces the grant to that organization.

Consent must say, in product language:

> Allow this agent to find and read documents you can access across Alderdocs, including documents shared with you. This includes workspaces you join and documents shared with you later. If you allow creation or editing, the agent can do those things wherever your current access permits them.

Offer separate permissions for reading, creating, and editing. A read-only client can request reading alone. A client asking for all tools must explain all three permissions. No agent permission includes inviting people, changing roles, sharing documents, deleting documents, moving documents, or managing billing.

| OAuth scope | Allows, subject to current access |
| --- | --- |
| `documents:read` | List and read accessible documents; discover member workspaces |
| `documents:create` | Create a document in an explicitly named writable workspace |
| `documents:update` | Replace content of an accessible editable document |

The normal authoring connection requests all three. Creation and update scopes do not imply reading; clients needing discovery also request `documents:read`. Unknown or absent scopes deny the corresponding operation. Browser sessions have their own authenticated authority and do not need agent consent, but use the same resource capability checks.

The grant deliberately follows current access rather than a frozen list of workspace IDs. Joining another workspace or accepting a direct share makes those resources reachable without reconnecting. Leaving a workspace, a role downgrade, or share removal takes effect on subsequent operations even while a token remains unexpired. Consent must explain this future-access behavior before approval. Selecting a library filter does not alter the grant. A future workspace allowlist would be an additional explicit grant restriction, not an interpretation of a UI filter.

Show each connected agent, its permissions, and a revoke action in account settings. Revocation disables the grant, refresh path, and existing access tokens at the product gate. Adding scopes requires new consent; refreshing cannot broaden them. Separate clients receive separate grants. Require a live account and live grant on each call, so account closure or revocation cannot be bypassed by an otherwise valid signed token.

Validate signature or introspection, issuer, expiry, subject, intended resource/audience, client/grant binding, and scopes through the managed provider's supported contract. Do not accept an arbitrary sign-in token as MCP authorization. Verify the actual token and refresh contracts during implementation. If the chosen provider cannot issue account-level MCP credentials with resource binding, select a supported managed integration before release; do not work around it with an unverified organization claim or a home-workspace token.

This intentionally departs from the repository skill's default of selecting one organization during OAuth and requiring an active organization for every session. Those defaults would undermine the brief's cross-client workflow and document-only sharing. Keep provider organizations for membership; use verified user identity plus resource-specific checks for authority. Account and direct-share views remain usable without an active organization.

## Shared authorization boundary

UI, MCP, and future API callers invoke the same product capabilities. Transport adapters validate input and translate outcomes; they do not own parallel permission logic.

For each operation:

1. Authenticate the stable user and establish that the account is live. For MCP, establish the live grant and required scope.
2. Resolve the target document by its stable ID, or the explicitly supplied workspace for creation. Caller-supplied IDs select a target; they grant nothing.
3. Establish current membership and role, or an accepted direct share for this exact document. Derive the operation's capability using the matrix above.
4. Enforce the grant restriction and resource capability together. Reject unknown capabilities. Never use the document creator's privileges, a session's active organization, or the workspace's paying owner as the acting identity.
5. For a write, enforce the destination owner's storage allowance and commit authorization, quota reservation, and the mutation consistently. Revalidate if an external membership check occurred before a transaction and its authority has expired or changed. Concurrent local revocation must serialize with writes. A revocation arriving after a completed operation cannot undo that operation.
6. Release only the authorized result. Any queued work carries the original principal, grant, and target and checks them again when it executes and when releasing protected output.

Authorization answers who may act; storage limits answer whether the write fits. Storage is charged to the paying owner of the document's workspace, including writes by Editors or agents. The 25-member cap is the same on every plan, includes the Owner, and does not reduce permissions for already admitted members because a storage plan changes. Direct shares are not workspace members and do not consume member slots; invitation abuse limits can be defined separately.

Never authorize an entire search result from one membership check. Search, count, snippets, pagination, cached content, and document delivery must all respect the same current visibility. Scope queries to authorized candidates and recheck before returning results. Cache by principal and permission state; invalidate on access changes. Private results must not enter shared public caches.

## MCP tool contracts

Keep a static tool catalog for all authenticated clients. Tool visibility and annotations help clients plan; they do not authorize execution. Keep the three existing tool names and add explicit creation and workspace discovery. New optional filters preserve the existing unfiltered list behavior.

| Tool | Input | Required scope | Target and behavior |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `list_documents` | `{ query?, cursor?, workspace_id?, access? }` | `documents:read` | Default: union of member-workspace documents and accepted direct shares, deduplicated by document ID. Optional workspace filter requires membership in that workspace. `access` is `all` or `shared_with_me`; the latter means direct shares from workspaces the person does not belong to. |
| `read_document` | `{ id }` | `documents:read` | Resolve this document's workspace internally; require document read capability. No workspace argument or active workspace required. |
| `update_document` | `{ id, content }` | `documents:update` | Resolve this document internally and require update capability. Replace content only. Never upsert a missing ID or accept ownership, workspace, or sharing fields. |
| `create_document` | `{ workspace_id, title, content, idempotency_key }` | `documents:create` | Require current Owner or Editor membership in this exact workspace. Create one document there; require all fields, allowing empty content. No destination default and no implicit workspace creation. |
| `list_workspaces` | `{ cursor? }` | `documents:read` | List only current member workspaces, including empty ones, with stable IDs, names, roles, and derived `can_create_document`. A directly shared document does not add its parent workspace to this list. |

Reject unknown fields and unsupported filter combinations. `access=shared_with_me` with `workspace_id` is invalid, since that view contains only nonmember shares. A workspace filter narrows a request; omitting it returns all currently accessible documents, as advertised by the list tool. It never supplies the destination of a later create call.

`list_workspaces` is necessary to discover an empty writable workspace before creating its first document. An agent can resolve a person's named destination through this list, then pass its stable ID. Duplicate workspace names require clarification from the available task context; never choose the first result or infer a destination from document text. If the caller cannot identify a destination, return `WORKSPACE_REQUIRED` with instructions to list workspaces and select one. Tools need no confirmation flags or mandatory person-in-the-loop step; a previously specified destination is sufficient.

List results return metadata rather than full document content: `id`, `title`, `updated_at`, canonical app `url`, `access_source` (`membership` or `direct_share`), and `can_update`. Member documents additionally include `workspace: { id, name }`. Nonmember direct shares omit parent-workspace identity and expose only the document's own metadata. `read_document` adds `content`; writes return the resulting document ID, allowed metadata, workspace ID for member callers, and URL only after the write durably commits. `can_update` and `can_create_document` describe the current caller, including token scopes, and remain hints subject to rechecking.

Use a fixed default and maximum page size of 50. Sort documents by `updated_at` descending, then ID, and workspaces by ID. Bind opaque cursors to subject, grant where applicable, search, filters, and sort. A cursor is a continuation position, not an authorization snapshot: recheck access on every page. Return no inaccessible counts or snippets. Access churn may shorten pages; concurrent edits may reorder results, so clients deduplicate by ID. A cursor from a different subject or query is invalid.

For creation, scope the idempotency key to principal and destination workspace, and record a payload fingerprint and resulting ID atomically with the write. Retain successful keys for at least 24 hours. A matching retry within that window returns the original result only after fresh authorization; a different payload returns `IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT`. Never create or charge storage twice for a retained key. After that window deduplication is not guaranteed; clients should inspect the library before repeating an uncertain old create. If a transport fails after commit, report the outcome as unknown and retry with the same key.

Declare list and read tools read-only. Creation is a write without replacement or deletion; update can replace existing content and must be annotated accordingly. Creation is retry-safe only under its documented idempotency contract. Do not claim broader tool guarantees from annotations.

### Errors and audit

Use the MCP authorization mechanism for invalid/expired credentials and insufficient token scopes. For authenticated product refusals, return typed outcomes and short corrective instructions:

- `NOT_FOUND_OR_NO_ACCESS`: same response for an unknown document and one the caller cannot read; disclose no title, workspace, or existence.
- `READ_ONLY`: only after establishing read access, explain that editing is unavailable.
- `WORKSPACE_REQUIRED` / `INVALID_INPUT`: name the missing or invalid field without silently choosing a target.
- `WORKSPACE_NOT_AVAILABLE`: same response for an unknown workspace and one without membership; do not disclose its name.
- `CREATE_NOT_ALLOWED`: for a known member workspace where the caller lacks creation rights.
- `STORAGE_LIMIT`: destination owner's storage allowance prevents the write; identify the authorized destination and next step.
- `INVALID_CURSOR`, `IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT`, or `AUTHORITY_UNAVAILABLE`: correct the request or retry once current access can be established. An authority outage must not appear as a successful empty library.

Audit writes and grant changes with stable actor, grant/client when delegated, document and workspace IDs, action, time, and outcome. Keep sensitive content and tokens out of logs. Agent writes remain attributable to the person and connected client; they do not become writes by the paying owner.

## Library behavior

Use `/documents` as the account-level library and `/documents/{id}` as the canonical document URL. Document IDs resolve their workspace internally, allowing a direct-share recipient to open a link without workspace membership. These routes intentionally replace the skill's workspace-nested routing default for this product's cross-workspace collection. A copied link grants no access.

On entry, show all readable documents in a single list sorted by most recently updated. Search spans that same scope, so studio leads can see changes across clients. Show a workspace label on each member document and **Shared with you** on a document-only share. Include the modified time. Do not expose the parent workspace of a document-only share.

Provide a library filter with **All documents**, each member workspace, and **Shared with you**. This is a view filter, not a global active-workspace switcher. Workspace settings may have their own targeted routes but do not change the library or agent authority.

Encode search and scope in the URL, for example:

- `/documents`: default all-accessible view.
- `/documents?q=proposal&workspace=<id>`: search within one member workspace.
- `/documents?access=shared_with_me`: nonmember direct shares.
- `/documents?access=all`: an explicitly chosen all-accessible view, preserved when shared or reloaded.

Use one parser/serializer; reject conflicting filters. Filter changes reset pagination and preserve the search text. Back, reload, and copied links restore the view under the recipient's own permissions. A stale or unauthorized workspace filter displays an unavailable-workspace state with a link to All documents; do not silently substitute another workspace. An empty valid workspace says it has no matching documents. A new account's workspace may be empty; direct-share-only users can browse without being forced into another organization.

### Creation in the library

**New document** opens a product dialog at `/documents?create=true` with existing filters preserved. It includes a visible required **Workspace** field containing only workspaces where the person can create, along with the title and content fields.

- In All documents or Shared with you, leave the destination unselected when multiple writable workspaces exist. Do not remember the last destination as a default.
- In a writable workspace-filtered view, preselect that workspace and keep its name visible and editable in the dialog.
- If exactly one writable workspace exists, preselect it visibly.
- In a read-only workspace view, offer **New document in another workspace** only if another writable destination exists, and require its explicit selection.
- If no writable workspace exists, hide the creation action and explain how to request Editor access in the relevant empty/onboarding state. Creating a workspace is a separate workflow.

The submit action says **Create in [workspace name]**. Recheck the destination at submission; if rights were lost or the workspace disappeared, preserve the draft and require a new choice. Do not reroute creation to the person's first workspace. Charge storage to the selected workspace's owner and show any limit failure in the dialog. On success, open the new document with its workspace label visible. The editor continues to show where the document lives without introducing an active-workspace authority.

Library data updates as permissions and documents change. Invalidate content and remove inaccessible rows from the current client state after revocation is observed; an open editor stops saving and shows an access-changed state. Rechecking the backend is mandatory even before the UI catches up. Previously delivered content cannot be recalled from an agent or browser.

## Assumptions and implementation checks

The following are decisions made without the owner present, and can be reviewed tomorrow without blocking the design:

1. Owners and Editors read, create, and update every document in their workspace; Viewers read only. There are no member-specific document exclusions in this version.
2. Direct email shares are read-only, survive loss of membership when independently valid, do not consume workspace member slots, and reveal no parent-workspace metadata. Pending invitations require verified acceptance.
3. Account-wide agent consent includes future workspaces and direct shares, with explicit disclosure. There is no workspace allowlist, separate workspace opt-in, or organization administrator agent veto in this version. If enterprise policy is later added, it must further restrict the grant at the shared gate.
4. Storage quota is charged to the destination workspace's paying owner. The member cap is a fixed 25 including the Owner, independent of plan; no plan-based seat eviction is introduced.
5. Creation requires a title, explicit content, and an idempotency key. The proposed discovery tool, optional list filters, result metadata, routes, and page sizes are new contracts; no existing implementation establishes them.
6. No public sharing, document moves, MCP deletion, sharing administration, or workspace creation is implied by these tools. Those require separate product capabilities if approved later.

Before implementation is called complete, verify the managed provider's actual account-level OAuth, resource binding, consent, refresh, membership freshness, and grant-revocation behavior. This is a provider integration check, not evidence of support in the current repository. Keep protocol details aligned with the supported MCP clients and provider documentation when implementing.

Acceptance cases for implementation:

- One agent grant lists documents in two client workspaces plus a direct share, deduplicates overlapping access, and can discover an empty writable workspace. A new workspace becomes reachable under the disclosed grant without reconnection.
- Viewer access and direct shares permit reading but reject update and create; an Editor succeeds only within their actual writable workspaces. Guessing an ID, using another subject's cursor, or supplying a workspace name never widens access.
- Membership loss, role downgrade, direct-share revocation, grant revocation, and account closure block the next applicable operation despite a valid old token. Independent remaining access paths still work. Provider outages fail closed.
- A read-only token cannot write even when its subject is an Owner. An editing token cannot create without creation scope. Missing, foreign-resource, expired, or revoked credentials fail before protected data is released.
- Creating without a destination fails; duplicate names require resolution. No cookie, provider active organization, library filter, or concurrent browser tab supplies or changes an MCP destination.
- Repeated concurrent creation with one idempotency key produces one document and one storage charge. A changed payload conflicts; revoked access prevents replay disclosure. Updating cannot upsert, reparent, or share.
- The library restores search and filters on reload and Back, defaults to the cross-workspace view, hides unauthorized metadata and actions, and handles empty, direct-share-only, stale-filter, storage-limit, and lost-access states.
- Creation from All documents requires a visible destination choice when ambiguous; filtered creation shows its destination; losing permission preserves the draft and never switches the target automatically.

This change is documentation only. These acceptance cases define the future implementation checks; they have not been run against a product runtime.
